														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2507061">
									

<p>Actually, I've realized &#8212; and discussed this with LordStonefish on IRC &#8212; that AWCY isn't even necessary here.</p>
<p>The barnacles are indicative of why. They allude to the phrase &quot;billions of blistering blue barnacles&quot;, which <em>only appeared in the English translation</em>. Escoffier was a francophone. Why would he have the English-language version instead of the French-language original?</p>
<p>He was a collector and a completionist. This is why he had the translated version &#8212; but also, it's why he <em>used</em> the translated version. He didn't want to use his prized original! It was a <em>first printing</em>! It was even <em>autographed</em>! If he sacrificed it in the ritual, it'd be ruined!</p>
<p>So instead of sacrificing to his gods something he truly valued and prized and cherished, he used something which he considered a barely-adequate substitute. Maybe he figured the gods wouldn't notice?</p>
<p>And now his gods are lobotomized blobs of flesh based on the English dialogue.</p>
<p>If you look at the original French, Capitaine Haddock's original swearing &#8212; the phrase which was translated to &quot;billions of blistering blue barnacles&quot; &#8212; was &quot;mille millions de mille sabords de tonnerre de Brest&quot;. That basically means he was swearing by <em>gunports from which cannons were firing</em>. Imagine what would have happened if <em>that</em> had formed the basis of the godbodies.</p>
<p>The godminds aren't trapped in the book <em>forever</em>, though. Just until the original summoning is complete. They just have to form &quot;billions of blistering blue barnacles&quot;, then they'll finally be free.</p>
<p>Literally, they have to form <em>billions</em> of barnacloid godbodies. A minimum of two billion. 2 * 10<sup>9</sup>.</p>
<p>And they can only form three at a time. And each triad takes ten minutes to form. And they can only form when the book is open to the right page.</p>
<p>The book would have to be open to that page for over eleven thousand years.</p>

							</div>

								
					
